= Quantum and Category-5 boundary

NIST Category 5 compares applicable attack resources with key search on a 256-bit block cipher. The retained criteria page gives preliminary references of 2-to-the-272 classical gates and 2-to-the-298 divided by MAXDEPTH quantum gates. These are accounting references, not certification of a field size.

A future candidate needs a complete polynomial scheme and exact real-key law; a QPT/QROM game with query, width, depth, gate, memory, data, target, preprocessing, and success accounting; an explicit advantage-to-EndRing-success inequality with loss and additive error; and independently reproduced classical and quantum attack margins. M1 has no eligible algorithms, losses, or parameters. M2 has a polynomial public break. Bit length alone is not Category-5 evidence.

= Novelty, procedure, and scope limits

Retained sources include NIST primary materials and abstract- or metadata-level records for Séta (ePrint 2019/1291), SiGamal (2020/613), LIT-SiGamal (2024/521), and supersingular key-updatable PKE (2020/1593). Full normalized constructions were unavailable for every neighbor. These sources identify comparison targets, not novelty; neither candidate establishes nonduplication.

The independence checker exited 1 with eight diagnostics. Two review files omit the nested task identifier in their attestations, so it does not recognize J1/J2, J5/J6, or blind-rederivation ownership; J7 belongs to later document review. That administrative failure is preserved and is not called a pass.

No experiment was run. Symbolic derivations are not timing measurements or attack lower bounds. The M2 result applies to the exact submitted syntax. M1's missing algorithm is not a proof of nonexistence.

Canonical graph check: knowledge/frontiers/ecdlp contains no affected graph. Historical research reports are immutable snapshots and were not edited.

= Exact next boundary

No automatic successor is authorized. A later proposal must jointly supply public encryption without the ring, an exact efficient operation available from the complete ring but not public finite data or a smaller witness, actual-message correctness on the exact distribution, and a QPT-compatible attacker-to-complete-EndRing reduction with explicit losses. A hash wrapper, authentication tag, or more evaluation points does not by itself change the failed recipient relation.

